course: dictionary
lesson: 7464
title: Porc: the pig on the menu
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Porc» is the French word for pig, and also for pork, the meat from a pig. Context tells you which one it means: «du porc» on a menu means some pork, the meat you eat.
explain: Look for clues. «After 1066 English kept its animal words (cow, pig, sheep) but took French meat words: beef from "bœuf", pork from "porc", mutton from "mouton".» The English word for the animal is pig, and the French-style word for the meat is pork, so porc is the meat.
explain: Use the swap check. Put «pig» in place of «porc» and see if the sentence still works, then try «pork». If the sentence is about eating, pork is the right meaning, not the animal.
fact: «Porc» means pig or pork, depending on the sentence.
fact: French is «le porc», «du porc» (some pork) and «rôti de porc» (roast pork).
fact: «Porc» is said roughly «por», with no «c» sound.
fact: English kept animal words like pig, cow and sheep, but took the French meat words pork, beef and mutton after the Norman Conquest.
exercise: «Beef comes from bœuf, pork from porc, mutton from mouton.» In this sentence, what does porc mean? | *Pork, the meat from a pig | Chicken | Cow | Sheep
exercise: On a French menu, «du porc» means what? | some cheese | *some pork | some beef | some bread
exercise: Which English word came from the French word «porc»? | cow | pig | sheep | *pork
test: How is «porc» said? | por-c, with a hard c sound like cat | *por, with no c sound | porr-ck | pork, with a k at the end
test: «The farmers kept English names for the animals, and the rich French speakers named the meat.» Who named the meat? | Cows and sheep | Children at school | *French-speaking nobles | English farmers
exam: Cow is to beef as pig is to what? | bread | mutton | veal | *pork
